src/backend/access/prolly/README

Prolly tree storage
===================

This directory holds prolly-tree storage for Postgres: table and index
access methods whose data lives in content-addressed trees, so that tables
can be branched, diffed, merged, cloned, pushed and pulled.  The design and
the plan are in specs/ at the top of the tree; start with
specs/README.md and specs/00-architecture-principles.md.

Status: roadmap step 4b, plus the canonical encoders (4a revisited).  Every interface exists.  The foundation
interfaces (node format, chunker, content hash, key and value encoders), the
cluster-wide node store and D1's WAL form have real implementations that
pass their conformance suites; everything else is a stub.  No table stores
data yet.


Interfaces and implementations
------------------------------

Every component is an interface with implementations behind it, so that any
design choice can be changed later by writing a new implementation:

	Interface         Header                  Implementations so far
	engine            prolly_engine.h         d1 d2 d3a d3b d4 (stubs)
	coordination      prolly_coord.h          k1 k2 k3 (stubs)
	node_format       prolly_nodeformat.h     basic, stub
	chunker           prolly_chunker.h        keyhash, stub
	content_hash      prolly_hash.h           sha256, stub
	key_encoder       prolly_keyenc.h         ordered, stub
	value_encoder     prolly_valenc.h         canonical, heaptuple, stub
	node_store        prolly_nodestore.h      global, stub
	allocator         prolly_alloc.h          stub
	reclamation       prolly_reclaim.h        stub
	log               prolly_log.h            chunks, stub
	commit_pipeline   prolly_commit.h         stub
	row_identity      prolly_rowid.h          stub
	refs              prolly_refs.h           stub
	transfer          prolly_transfer.h       stub
	diff_merge        prolly_merge.h          stub

An interface header declares a routine struct: a ProllyImplInfo (interface,
name, impl_id, format_version) followed only by callback pointers, with the
contract written as comments.  An implementation is one or more source files
here, named <interface>_<implementation>.c, that define a const routine
struct.  Nothing else includes an implementation's private headers or calls
its functions.

Callers go through PROLLY_CALL(routine, callback, args...) (prolly_impl.h).
A NULL callback means "not implemented", and PROLLY_CALL raises
ERRCODE_FEATURE_NOT_SUPPORTED naming the interface, implementation and
callback.  So a stub is a routine with every callback NULL, and a real
implementation can be filled in one callback at a time.

Built-in implementations are listed in src/include/access/prolly_impllist.h,
the one place that names them, and found by interface and name through the
registry (prolly_registry.c).  Libraries in shared_preload_libraries can
register more with prolly_register_impl().

Which implementation a tree uses comes from configuration (prolly_config.c),
never from code.  Today that is a default profile per table AM:

	Table AM      Engine   Coordination
	prolly_d1     d1       k1
	prolly_d2     d2       k1
	prolly_d3a    d3a      k2
	prolly_d3b    d3b      k2
	prolly_d4     d4       k3

All five share the same foundation: node format basic, chunker keyhash,
content hash sha256, key encoder ordered, value encoder canonical, and stubs
for the interfaces without an implementation yet.  prolly_d1 uses the node
store global and the log chunks; prolly_d2 the node store global.


Canonical values
----------------

Rows and keys are stored in canonical form: the same bytes on every
platform, in every cluster and session, whatever the table's DDL history,
with no local OIDs (specs/09-versioned-catalogs.md).  prolly_datum.c holds
the canonical encoding of single values, shared by the key and value
encoders as a fixed part of the format: send formats produced with the
client encoding fixed to UTF-8 (SetClientEncodingOverride in mbutils.c),
and OID-free encodings of arrays and composites.  valenc_canonical.c stores
live columns only, without trailing NULLs.  heaptuple stays registered for
comparison and fails the platform and history checks by design.


Storage and WAL
---------------

All nodes, of every relation in every database on every branch, live in one
cluster-wide store (nodestore_global.c): relation files 9393 (nodes), 9394
(hash -> block map) and 9395 (map overflow) in the global tablespace,
through smgr and the buffer manager.  Internal nodes carry an unhashed
block hint per child, so reads usually skip the map.

Two WAL resource managers carry prolly records (prolly_xlog.h,
prolly_rmgr.c): Prolly for the log interface (D1's "chunks" logs each new
node as a full page image) and ProllyStore for the node store's own map.
Each record names the implementation that wrote it, and redo dispatches to
it.  test_prolly's TAP test replays both on a streaming standby and through
crash recovery under wal_consistency_checking.

Database-wide components (refs, transfer, diff_merge) come from a database
profile.  Once a tree can be created, its components will be recorded in the
tree with their format stamps and read back from there, so changing
configuration never changes how existing data is read.


Adapters
--------

The Postgres-facing code is thin and holds no engine logic:

	prolly_tableam.c   one TableAmRoutine for all prolly table AMs
	prolly_indexam.c   the "prolly" index AM; its opclasses follow the btree
	                   contract, so validation is btvalidate()
	prolly_vcs.c       SQL functions prolly_branch_create, prolly_checkout,
	                   prolly_commit, prolly_diff, prolly_merge, prolly_clone,
	                   prolly_fetch, prolly_push

Table AM callbacks that map directly onto an engine callback (storage
creation, truncation, copy, vacuum) already call through the engine
interface.  The rest raise "does not implement ... yet" until their
translation is designed.

The catalog entries (pg_am, pg_proc, pg_opfamily, pg_opclass, pg_amop,
pg_amproc) use OIDs 9370-9392.


Tests
-----

src/test/modules/test_prolly runs each interface's conformance suite
against every registered implementation, and reaches each stub from SQL:

	make -C src/test/modules/test_prolly check
